map.claim1_selector:
Claim 1 is not taken, but it is narrower than the draft says. Every published router found decides before the LLM answers, from the encoder's uncertainty or from the input. EnergyRoute: Helmholtz energy, thresholds as percentiles of the test batch's energy, verified. L2D-Clinical, Arora, CMBS and Emer do the same. Several fuse the outputs rather than pick one. Two works do use the rarity of the proposed label. Li Z et al. 2512.21041 sends MacBERT's answer to people when the predicted code is under 5% of samples or confidence is under 0.6; this is a fixed rule over 12 codes with one classifier. STREAQ is binary. LCA/BKS judge a classifier by the class it outputs.

What remains new is the combination:
- a learned per-coder selector over three coders' final answers (encoder, zero-shot LLM, retrieval LLM), with training support as its main input;
- frozen on one year and scored over whole later years at natural proportions;
- on two datasets with three LLMs.

EnergyRoute is the closest work and is not in references.bib. Its test is 500 stratified 2021 items and its thresholds come from the test batch, so 'temporal test' alone is not new and 'frozen' is.

The deciding experiment uses the frozen 2026 year, scored by macro-F1 overall and by frequency bin. Compare the selector against:
- (a) EnergyRoute-style energy gating with the encoder, kNN and retrieval-LLM tiers;
- (b) an LCA/BKS table of per-code validation precision for each coder;
- (c) the Li Z rule (rare-code-or-low-confidence goes to the LLM);
- (d) an L2D-style logistic deferral on confidence only;
- (e) the selector with the support feature removed.

If support beats (b) and (e) and the selector beats (a) on both datasets, the claim stands as an increment. If (b) matches it, the contribution shrinks to an evaluation finding. The manuscript's line that training support 'has no counterpart' must go.
